from review_schema import A

A('7:1.1','Under the Wave off Kanagawa — The Great Wave','R',{'Process':'Japanese colour woodblock print: relief printing uses aligned impressions from carved blocks. Exact block count and impression state cannot be derived from the slide.','Composition & Motifs':'The enormous wave curls from upper left over three narrow boats with bent rowers. White foam branches like claws, while tiny snow-topped Fuji sits near the lower centre of the distant horizon. A title cartouche and signature occupy upper left.','Edition & Circulation':'The module treats Japanese prints as portable objects collected and reinterpreted in Europe.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Japanese landscape and labour are represented through Japanese printmaking; later European responses may borrow composition while using other media.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme relief-print registration',sig='visual/aesthetic=Scale contrast, asymmetry and a flat blue-white pattern create dramatic movement.|economic/trade=The lecture links print circulation to European collecting and artistic exchange.',dist='The mountain is the small distant Fuji, not the cresting foreground wave.|The image is a designed print, not a photographic record of a specific wave.')
A('7:2.1','Figure by a tree and mountain — ukiyo-e video image','P',{'Composition':'A small seated figure rests against a sloping tree trunk in front of a large mountain. Hanging branches descend from above; the video title obscures much of the lower image.','Palette & Pigment':'Muted ochre mountain, grey-blue trunk and subdued green ground; the thumbnail cannot establish original pigments.','Line & Hand':'Fine print or brush detail is too blurred to settle the source medium. The painting/manuscript category is provisional for the uncaptioned pictorial source.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme',sig='visual/aesthetic=The small figure and large mountain introduce an asymmetrical Japanese landscape arrangement.',dist='The phrase Introduction to Ukiyo-e titles the video; it is not a title or secure medium identification for the reproduced artwork.',uncertain=['Original work, artist, date and whether the source is painting or print are unresolved.'])
A('7:3.1','Lake Chuzenji in Shimotsuke Province','R',{'Process':'Colour woodblock-print context; separate colour fields, key outlines and graded blue water are visible. Matrix count is not known.','Composition & Motifs':'Two massive foreground tree trunks cut vertically through a lake and mountain view. A torii stands near the lower-right shoreline; small travellers descend beside a railing at right; tiny boats and lakeside buildings appear at left. Red title labels occupy the upper right.','Edition & Circulation':'A place-specific Japanese print becomes part of the module’s account of European access to Japanese imagery.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme relief-print',sig='visual/aesthetic=Foreground obstruction and abrupt scale change offer alternatives to an unobstructed central vista.',dist='The trunks are deliberate framing devices, not defects obscuring the intended subject.')
A('7:4.1','Courtesan — Keisai Eisen','R',{'Process':'Japanese colour woodblock print; fine dark contours enclose large patterned garment areas.','Composition & Motifs':'A tall turning figure fills a narrow sheet. Numerous hairpins radiate around the coiffure. A large dragon and curling waves decorate the dark robe; signature marks lie low at left.','Edition & Circulation':'The caption identifies this as the source image compared with Van Gogh’s copy.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'The original Japanese subject and print format are transferred into a European painted adaptation.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme dragon',sig='visual/aesthetic=Garment pattern, contour and vertical format are central resources for the adaptation.',dist='Eisen’s print is not Van Gogh’s painting, even where the figure is closely copied.',compare=[{'target':'Courtesan after Eisen — Van Gogh','relationship':'derivative','reason':'The lecture explicitly calls Van Gogh’s image a copy of this Japanese print.'}])
A('7:4.2','Courtesan after Eisen — Van Gogh','P',{'Composition':'A copied tall figure is enclosed in a bright yellow inner rectangle; an added outer field contains water, reeds, birds and other creatures.','Palette & Pigment':'Strong yellow, red, blue and green replace the original’s more restricted tones; conspicuous strokes animate the background.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'Long-legged birds stand at left, lily pads and flowers run down the water margin, and a frog-like creature occupies the bottom centre. The dragon robe motif is retained but coarsened into painted colour.','Line & Hand':'Thick dark contours and visibly separated strokes translate the print’s linear structure into painting.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A Japanese courtesan image is rendered in a European artist’s painting; copying a woodblock design does not make the new work a woodblock print.'},'japonisme ukiyo-e dragon',sig='visual/aesthetic=Added border and intensified colour make adaptation distinguishable from exact facsimile.',dist='Derivative describes the acknowledged source relationship; the painted border and changed palette show active transformation.',compare=[{'target':'Courtesan — Keisai Eisen','relationship':'derivative','reason':'Copied figure and garment design, altered medium and surrounding imagery.'}])
A('7:5.1;8:20.3','Portrait of Père Tanguy','P',{'Composition':'The seated frontal man forms a broad central triangle with folded hands. Japanese images cover the wall behind him, cropping around his shoulders and hat.','Palette & Pigment':'Blue-green coat, warm face and bright yellow-orange background contrast through separate directional strokes.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'A mountain image appears above the hat; female figures flank both shoulders and the lower-right edge; floral imagery occupies the lower left.','Line & Hand':'Short strokes model the face and jacket, unlike the flatter reproduced prints behind him.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A French sitter is painted by Van Gogh among Japanese images: collecting becomes part of portraiture rather than proof the sitter is Japanese.'},'japonisme collecting print-transfer',sig='social=The sitter is located within a culture of collecting and circulating images.|visual/aesthetic=The portrait combines a bodily foreground with flat reproduced backgrounds.|regional-identity=Module 8 returns to this work as evidence of cultural connectedness.',dist='The background expresses an artist’s collected and imagined Japan; it does not depict a Japanese room observed on location.')
for refs,title,desc in [('7:6.1','Sunflowers — lecture example','A frontal vase sits on a horizontal tabletop against a yellow field. Upright flowers, drooping heads and heavy seed centres form an uneven oval above it.'),('7:6.2','Almond Blossoms','Branches spread diagonally across an uninterrupted blue sky, cropped on every side. Pale blossoms and dark-edged twigs form an all-over surface without a horizon.')]:
 A(refs,title,'P',{'Composition':desc,'Line & Hand':'Separate directional strokes and assertive outlines remain visible; physical paint thickness cannot be measured from this reproduction.','Palette & Pigment':'Sunflowers emphasizes yellow-orange against yellow; Almond Blossoms sets pale flowers against turquoise-blue. These colours do not establish pigment chemistry.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Van Gogh’s painted botanical motifs are discussed through his engagement with Japanese pictorial design, not as Japanese-made prints.'},'japonisme floral-trade',sig='visual/aesthetic=Colour fields, strong contour and cropped plant forms support the module’s discussion of Japanese-inspired seeing.',dist='The heading’s Arles 1888 date does not date Almond Blossoms, which the caption gives as 1890.|A shared floral subject alone does not prove direct copying of one Japanese print.')
A('7:7.1','Portrait of Émile Zola','P',{'Composition':'Zola sits at left with an open illustrated book, turned toward a desk crowded with papers and books. A Japanese-style screen stands at far left; three reproduced pictures occupy the upper-right wall.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'The screen includes a small bird on a flowering branch. A feather quill rises from an ornamental desk vessel; a blue booklet visibly bears Manet’s name. The wall grouping contains Olympia, a Japanese standing figure and a partly obscured monochrome group.','Palette & Pigment':'A dark suit and wall contrast with pale pages and face; the reproductions create lighter inset rectangles.','Text–Image Relationship':'Books and reproductions characterize the sitter’s intellectual and artistic associations.'},'japonisme collecting print-transfer',sig='social=Reading, writing and collecting furnish the writer’s public identity.|visual/aesthetic=Images within the image connect the portrait to several artistic traditions.',dist='The slide’s enlarged right-hand crop is a detail of the same portrait, not a second painting.')
for refs,title,desc in [('7:7.2','Olympia reproduction inside Zola’s portrait','A reclining nude lies on white bedding in a dark horizontal rectangle at the front of the wall grouping; an attendant stands at right.'),('7:7.3','Japanese standing-figure print inside Zola’s portrait','At the left of the wall grouping a bulky standing figure wears a dark outer garment over a patterned brown robe against blue.'),('7:7.4','Monochrome group reproduction inside Zola’s portrait','At the upper right, several heads and upper bodies remain visible above and behind the Olympia reproduction.')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Composition & Motifs':desc,'Process':'A represented reproduction within Manet’s painting, not a separately photographed original print. Its exact printing technique cannot be read from the painted surrogate.','Edition & Circulation':'The wall arrangement demonstrates association and collecting; component identities beyond Olympia are not individually captioned in the lecture.'},'collecting print-transfer japonisme',sig='visual/aesthetic=The inset becomes evidence of artistic reference within a portrait.',dist='Do not treat the painted depiction as an independently inspected physical print or use it to establish an edition.',uncertain=['Separate print metadata and, for unnamed components, catalogue identification need verification.'])
A('7:8.1','Water Lilies — Art Institute example','P',{'Composition':'Floating pads and blossoms gather around a large open blue patch of reflected water. No sky horizon or shore anchors the view.','Perspective Convention':'The viewer looks down at water while reflections imply vertical surroundings; surface and depth remain deliberately ambiguous.','Line & Hand':'Broken, overlapping touches define pads, flowers and blue-green-violet reflections.','Palette & Pigment':'Cool blue and green dominate with small pink-white flowers.'},'japonisme surface-depth',sig='visual/aesthetic=The horizonless field encourages attention to pattern and reflection rather than a conventional distant vista.',dist='The Art Institute painting and MoMA’s wide multi-panel work are separate works sharing a series title.',compare=[{'target':'Water Lilies — MoMA multi-panel work','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Slide 8 compares distinct versions and scales.'}])
A('7:8.2','Water Lilies — MoMA multi-panel work','P',{'Composition':'A very wide, multi-panel field stretches horizontally across the photographed gallery wall. Patches of lilies and reflected foliage alternate with blue-violet open water.','Perspective Convention':'The expansive surface has no single horizon or central focal object.','Line & Hand':'Broad passages and scattered marks merge at viewing distance; the gallery photograph does not resolve every brushstroke.','Palette & Pigment':'Blue-green fields alternate with pale pink, cream and violet passages.'},'surface-depth japonisme',sig='visual/aesthetic=Width and continuous surface create a different encounter from the smaller painting beside it.',dist='Do not apply the 1897–99 date of the left-hand Art Institute example to the undated MoMA work.',compare=[{'target':'Water Lilies — Art Institute example','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Same series subject, different objects, scales and display contexts.'}])
A('','MoMA Water Lilies gallery arrangement','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'The wide painting spans the far wall above a low barrier. Three dark rounded seats occupy the open wooden floor; a seated visitor at left gives bodily scale. Ceiling tracks direct light toward the painting.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'The uncluttered floor and seating permit prolonged viewing at a distance, while the painting’s width extends beyond a narrow frontal glance. This is an inference from the visible arrangement.','Objects & Origins':'Monet’s Water Lilies is shown in a modern museum gallery; the slide does not date the installation photograph.'},'museum-display surface-depth',sig='visual/aesthetic=Seating, scale and an open approach shape a sustained panoramic encounter.',dist='Display furniture and barriers are part of the museum installation, not Monet’s painted composition.',slides='7:8')
for refs,title,desc in [('7:9.1','Jane Avril — Toulouse-Lautrec','A dancer lifts a black-stockinged leg within a sweeping red-orange skirt at upper left. A vastly enlarged instrument neck and musician’s hand frame the lower right, while diagonal floorboards recede behind.'),('7:9.2','Divan Japonais — Toulouse-Lautrec','A black-clad woman in a large hat dominates the seated foreground beside a top-hatted man at right. The stage performer is cropped at upper left; musicians and instruments fill the space between audience and stage.')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':'Colour lithographic poster context; flat colour areas and bold contour are visible, but stone count requires catalogue evidence.','Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'Large title lettering identifies entertainment and celebrity in a reproducible urban advertisement.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Parisian nightlife is represented in European lithography with compositional devices discussed under Japanese influence.'},'japonisme lithography poster',sig='economic/trade=The poster advertises commercial entertainment.|visual/aesthetic=Cropping, silhouettes and strong diagonals organize attention at a glance.',dist='Japanese-inspired composition does not change the subject into a Japanese performance or make the process Japanese woodblock printing.')
A('7:10.1','Camille Monet in Japanese Costume — La Japonaise','P',{'Composition':'A turning woman in a long red robe fills the canvas while round fans scatter across the wall and floor. The robe’s train curves across the foreground.','Figural Style':'The woman looks back toward the viewer and raises an open fan. Her light hair contrasts with the theatrical Japanese garment.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'An embroidered warrior with sword occupies the robe’s lower front; birds and plants cover the upper red fabric. Fans contain miniature landscapes and figures.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A European sitter performs a fashionable imagined Japan through costume and collected objects in a European painting.'},'japonisme costume orientalism-occidentalism',sig='social=Costume and fans make European consumption of Japanese visual culture visible.|visual/aesthetic=The robe turns a three-dimensional body into a broad patterned field.',dist='The wearer is Camille Monet, not an anonymous Japanese woman.|The slide says 1875; this date needs checking against the holding museum’s catalogue.',uncertain=['Slide date requires museum verification.'])
A('7:10.2','The Letter — Mary Cassatt','R',{'Process':'Colour print; its specific intaglio techniques must be distinguished from the Japanese woodblock comparison. Flat colour does not prove woodcut.','Composition & Motifs':'A woman in a blue patterned garment bends toward a white envelope at her mouth. A sharply angled writing surface carries another sheet; pale floral wallpaper fills the background.','Edition & Circulation':'The work belongs to Cassatt’s engagement with Japanese prints through a different printmaking practice.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A European domestic subject is rendered by an American artist using intaglio methods informed by Japanese print aesthetics.'},'japonisme aquatint print-transfer',sig='visual/aesthetic=Patterned surfaces and a close cropped domestic action replace a deep narrative setting.|social=The everyday activity of correspondence is treated as a principal subject.',dist='The 1893 After the Bath print postdates Cassatt’s 1890–91 work and cannot be its direct source.',compare=[{'target':'After the Bath — Woman of the Kansei Era','relationship':'parallel','reason':'The slide illustrates formal affinities; chronology excludes direct derivation from this later print.'}])
A('7:10.3','After the Bath — Woman of the Kansei Era','R',{'Process':'Japanese colour-print context with outlined figures, restrained flat colours and delicate pattern; precise block count is unknown.','Composition & Motifs':'A woman in a pale patterned robe stands by an open threshold and touches a cloth to her face. A small dog with a red collar stands at lower right; a pine and railing appear behind her. Title and signature panels occupy the right edge.','Edition & Circulation':'The caption dates the print 1893 while its subject refers to an earlier era.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A Japanese domestic subject and Japanese print are compared with Cassatt’s European domestic scene.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme relief-print',sig='visual/aesthetic=Threshold cropping, patterned cloth and intimate gesture support the paired comparison.',dist='The Kansei era in the subject title is not the print’s production date.',compare=[{'target':'The Letter — Mary Cassatt','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Formal comparison across separate media and dates.'}])
A('7:11.1','Under Mannen Bridge at Fukagawa','R',{'Process':'Japanese colour woodblock print, with sharp outlines and graded blue water and sky.','Composition & Motifs':'A high arched bridge spans the upper image on two tall posts. Tiny people cross its crest; boats move underneath; distant Fuji is framed within the bridge opening. A title block stands at upper left.','Edition & Circulation':'The lecture pairs the print with Monet’s Japanese bridge; its captioned 1823 date is a source datum requiring catalogue checking.'},'ukiyo-e japonisme relief-print',sig='visual/aesthetic=The large arch frames a distant landmark and converts infrastructure into a dominant graphic curve.',dist='A bridge-shaped curve in two images establishes a comparison, not proof Monet copied this exact sheet.',compare=[{'target':'Water Lilies and Japanese Bridge — Monet','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Explicit paired motif comparison.'}],uncertain=['1823 slide date needs verification.'])
A('7:11.2','Water Lilies and Japanese Bridge — Monet','P',{'Composition':'A pale curved bridge crosses the middle of dense greenery. Its reflection and the lily-covered pond fill the lower half; hanging foliage descends from above.','Line & Hand':'Short broken strokes intermingle foliage, water and reflected structure, contrasting with Hokusai’s more sharply bounded shapes.','Palette & Pigment':'Green and blue dominate, punctuated by pale bridge rails and pink-white blossoms.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A Japanese-inspired garden bridge is represented in Monet’s painting; the process remains painting rather than Japanese relief printing.'},'japonisme surface-depth',sig='visual/aesthetic=The bridge links designed garden structure and painterly reflection.',dist='Monet’s bridge has a different scale, setting and function from the populated urban bridge in Hokusai’s print.',compare=[{'target':'Under Mannen Bridge at Fukagawa','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Both compositions place a curved bridge over water: Hokusai uses a populated urban setting, while Monet surrounds the bridge with garden planting.'}])
for refs,title,desc in [('7:12.1','Claude Monet — photographic portrait in video','A bearded man in a hat stands beside a lily pond, one hand near his hip. Garden vegetation and water place the portrait outdoors.'),('7:12.2','Mary Cassatt — photographic portrait in video','A seated woman in a dark dress rests her hands in her lap and lowers her eyes against a plain backdrop.')]:
 A(refs,title,'F',{'Subject & Evidence':desc,'Studio vs Documentary Context':'The video labels the sitter, but photographer, photographic date and original process are absent. Tape and film-strip graphics belong to the video design, not necessarily the original photographs.'},'photographic-mediation',sig='social=Portraits personify the artists discussed in the lecture.',dist='The overlaid label identifies the sitter; it does not supply the photograph’s maker or date.',uncertain=['Original photographic metadata uncaptioned.'])
A('7:13.1','Geisha’s Dancing — Beato studio attribution','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'A standing woman at left holds two open fans, one raised and one lowered. A seated woman at right plays a long-necked string instrument. Tatami-like flooring, shelves and a hanging landscape frame the interior.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'The posed grouping and arranged furnishings are consistent with a studio genre scene; the lecture captions a hand-coloured albumen print and credits Beato. It is evidence of photographic representation, not an unmediated sample of everyday life.','Material & Colour':'Applied pink, yellow and patterned textile colour overlays a photograph; hand-colouring is distinct from colour-film capture.'},'albumen photographic-mediation orientalism-occidentalism',sig='economic/trade=The lecture places photographs within circulating representations of Japan.|social=Studio staging and colouring mediate the performers’ presentation.|visual/aesthetic=Fans, costume and interior framing produce an organized tableau.',dist='The seated woman is playing an instrument, not performing the same standing dance.|A hand-coloured albumen photograph is not a chromolithograph.')
A('7:14.1','View of Tokyo — nineteenth-century photograph','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'An elevated view follows a broad street through low tiled roofs toward a distant horizon. Tiny pedestrians and scattered taller buildings articulate the city.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'An urban documentary viewpoint, though photographer, date and colouring method are not captioned. Selection of viewpoint remains an act of representation.'},'photographic-mediation orientalism-occidentalism',sig='social=The image introduces urban Japan beyond the studio tableau.|political=The slide asks about Westernisation in relation to an Orientalist view.',dist='A view of low roofs is not proof of an unchanging or wholly premodern Japan.',uncertain=['Exact place, photographer, process and date remain unspecified.'])
A('7:14.2','French — Issen Yoshikazu','R',{'Process':'Polychrome print, captioned ōban size. A Japanese printmaking context represents Europeans and their camera.','Composition & Motifs':'A man in dark coat and pale trousers bends beneath a cloth at a tripod camera on the right. A bonneted woman stands behind; small bottles and equipment sit on a table at lower left. Script runs down the upper and lower right.','Edition & Circulation':'The caption gives 1861 and a Japanese collection, situating representation within encounter with foreign residents and technologies.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'European photographic practice becomes the subject of a Japanese printed image.'},'ukiyo-e photographic-mediation orientalism-occidentalism',sig='social=The picture reverses the direction of looking by making Europeans the represented subject.|visual/aesthetic=Clothing, equipment and posture identify a technological activity.',dist='This is a print depicting photography, not a photograph taken by the man it represents.')
A('','Japonisme — appreciation, collecting and appropriation','C',{'Definition':'In this module, Japonisme names European engagement with Japanese art, especially prints: collecting objects, copying images and adapting cropping, pattern, contour and spatial organization.','Nearest Confusable Terms':'Ukiyo-e is a Japanese pictorial tradition, not the name for its European reception. Orientalism asks broader questions about representing and idealizing an imagined East. Aesthetic influence does not always entail a change of medium.','Applied To':'Eisen–Van Gogh is a direct source comparison; Cassatt–Mizuno and Hokusai–Monet are parallel comparisons whose chronology and media must remain distinct.'},'japonisme ukiyo-e orientalism-occidentalism',dist='Japonisme is not simply any picture containing a Japanese object, nor evidence that its European maker travelled to Japan.',slides='7:1,4,5,6,7,9,10,11,13,14')
